1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,610
Key West has no rivers.

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:03,530
No lakes.

3
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:05,890
And almost no fresh water underground.

4
00:00:06,546 --> 00:00:09,176
And yet about twenty-five
thousand people live here,

5
00:00:09,366 --> 00:00:11,316
and every one of them can turn on a tap.

6
00:00:11,870 --> 00:00:13,660
So where does the water come from?

7
00:00:14,179 --> 00:00:15,649
Here's the wild part.

8
00:00:15,819 --> 00:00:18,139
It comes from a hundred
and thirty miles away,

9
00:00:18,179 --> 00:00:22,529
through one pipe, hopping across
the ocean on dozens of bridges.

10
00:00:23,148 --> 00:00:26,098
Your first guess might be:
they just collect the rain.

11
00:00:26,788 --> 00:00:29,338
Key West does get about
three feet of rain a year.

12
00:00:29,588 --> 00:00:33,338
But a normal roof catches nowhere
near enough for a modern family.

13
00:00:33,936 --> 00:00:37,506
Second guess: they turn
seawater into drinking water.

14
00:00:37,596 --> 00:00:38,296
They can!

15
00:00:38,336 --> 00:00:41,566
But most days, those
machines are switched off.

16
00:00:42,045 --> 00:00:46,075
So how does a tiny island at the very
end of the road keep its showers running?

17
00:00:46,545 --> 00:00:48,295
We'll go back to the days of rain barrels,

18
00:00:48,365 --> 00:00:51,055
ride a railroad across
the sea, follow the pipe,

19
00:00:51,205 --> 00:00:54,715
survive a few hurricanes, and find
out what happens after you flush.

20
00:00:55,185 --> 00:00:55,795
Let's go!

21
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:02,920
For most of its history,
Key West drank the sky.

22
00:01:03,525 --> 00:01:08,135
Every house had a cistern: a big tank
under the house or right next to it.

23
00:01:08,405 --> 00:01:11,595
When it rained, water ran off
the roof, through the gutters,

24
00:01:11,705 --> 00:01:12,675
and into the tank.

25
00:01:12,985 --> 00:01:15,035
That was your drinking
water for the dry months.

26
00:01:15,626 --> 00:01:18,826
Some families even floated a
thin film of kerosene on top,

27
00:01:18,866 --> 00:01:21,036
to stop mosquitoes breeding in it.

28
00:01:21,386 --> 00:01:21,696
Yum.

29
00:01:22,236 --> 00:01:23,626
And people got clever.

30
00:01:24,136 --> 00:01:27,066
Brackish water from shallow
wells went into the toilets.

31
00:01:27,636 --> 00:01:29,026
Salt water fought the fires.

32
00:01:29,496 --> 00:01:32,006
Fresh rainwater was way
too precious for that.

33
00:01:32,564 --> 00:01:33,594
And do the math.

34
00:01:33,824 --> 00:01:37,944
A thousand-square-foot roof in the Keys
catches around twenty-two thousand gallons

35
00:01:37,984 --> 00:01:38,934
in a year.

36
00:01:39,364 --> 00:01:42,254
Even a small, careful
household needs more than that.

37
00:01:42,524 --> 00:01:44,794
So even a perfect cistern could run dry.

38
00:01:45,387 --> 00:01:49,667
In the eighteen-sixties, the Army even
shipped in a high-tech British machine to

39
00:01:49,707 --> 00:01:52,357
boil seawater into fresh
water at Fort Taylor.

40
00:01:52,687 --> 00:01:54,977
It made about seven
thousand gallons a day.

41
00:01:55,527 --> 00:01:58,067
That's about what twenty
American homes use today.

42
00:01:58,107 --> 00:01:59,697
For a whole fort.

43
00:02:00,397 --> 00:02:01,987
And when the rain didn't come?

44
00:02:02,457 --> 00:02:05,157
Water had to arrive by boat, in big tanks,

45
00:02:05,197 --> 00:02:06,327
from the mainland.

46
00:02:06,737 --> 00:02:09,367
Which was fine, until
the town kept growing.

47
00:02:11,924 --> 00:02:15,534
Then came one of the craziest
building projects in American history.

48
00:02:16,166 --> 00:02:21,226
Henry Flagler, an oil tycoon who'd already
built hotels and railroads all down

49
00:02:21,266 --> 00:02:24,836
Florida, decided to run a
railroad across the sea,

50
00:02:25,386 --> 00:02:27,676
island to island, all the way to Key West.

51
00:02:28,323 --> 00:02:31,393
Building it in the middle of
the ocean had one big problem.

52
00:02:31,863 --> 00:02:35,893
Thousands of workers, tons of
concrete, and almost no fresh water.

53
00:02:36,303 --> 00:02:38,733
His crews drilled wells
all along the Keys.

54
00:02:39,143 --> 00:02:40,473
Most of them hit salt water.

55
00:02:40,977 --> 00:02:43,207
So the railroad hauled water in.

56
00:02:43,477 --> 00:02:46,787
Tank cars full of it, rolling out
over the sea from the mainland,

57
00:02:47,157 --> 00:02:49,927
millions of gallons for the
workers and for the concrete.

58
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:54,230
In January nineteen twelve, the
first train rolled into Key West,

59
00:02:54,270 --> 00:02:56,080
and the crowds went wild.

60
00:02:56,530 --> 00:02:59,360
For the first time, the island
was tied to the mainland.

61
00:03:00,027 --> 00:03:01,237
But not for long.

62
00:03:01,747 --> 00:03:06,267
On Labor Day, nineteen thirty-five, the
most intense hurricane ever to hit the

63
00:03:06,307 --> 00:03:08,737
United States tore through the Upper Keys.

64
00:03:09,027 --> 00:03:10,837
It swept away miles of track.

65
00:03:11,375 --> 00:03:13,265
The railroad was never rebuilt.

66
00:03:13,595 --> 00:03:14,905
The water trains stopped.

67
00:03:15,275 --> 00:03:17,845
And the old rail bridges
were turned into a road:

68
00:03:18,115 --> 00:03:19,225
the Overseas Highway.

69
00:03:21,798 --> 00:03:23,988
So who finally fixed Key West's water?

70
00:03:24,398 --> 00:03:24,968
The Navy.

71
00:03:25,578 --> 00:03:28,228
When the Navy base reopened
before World War Two,

72
00:03:28,438 --> 00:03:31,408
it needed about seventy-five
thousand gallons a day.

73
00:03:31,738 --> 00:03:35,198
Its own little seawater plant
made thirty-five thousand.

74
00:03:35,238 --> 00:03:36,208
Not even half.

75
00:03:36,787 --> 00:03:38,497
So the Navy did something bold.

76
00:03:38,827 --> 00:03:41,277
It paid two-thirds of
the bill for a pipeline.

77
00:03:42,047 --> 00:03:44,717
A hundred and twenty-eight miles
of eighteen-inch steel pipe,

78
00:03:44,987 --> 00:03:47,157
from wells in Florida
City on the mainland,

79
00:03:47,447 --> 00:03:49,337
all the way down the Keys to Key West.

80
00:03:49,911 --> 00:03:51,941
In some places the pipe was buried.

81
00:03:52,271 --> 00:03:54,461
At thirteen channels it went underwater.

82
00:03:54,991 --> 00:03:57,401
Everywhere else, it simply
rode along the bridges.

83
00:03:58,054 --> 00:04:00,544
On September twenty-second,
nineteen forty-two,

84
00:04:01,074 --> 00:04:03,264
the first pipeline water reached Key West.

85
00:04:03,819 --> 00:04:08,669
And here's a fun detail: a single drop
took about six days to make the whole trip.

86
00:04:09,162 --> 00:04:12,852
That first pipe held about ten
million gallons at any moment.

87
00:04:13,542 --> 00:04:17,582
Picture fifteen Olympic swimming pools,
squeezed into a skinny tube a hundred and

88
00:04:17,622 --> 00:04:19,272
twenty-eight miles long.

89
00:04:19,883 --> 00:04:22,193
But eighteen inches
wasn't enough for long.

90
00:04:22,563 --> 00:04:25,913
By the fifties, so many people
had moved down that on weekends,

91
00:04:26,243 --> 00:04:28,013
the water pressure basically vanished.

92
00:04:28,363 --> 00:04:31,133
If you lived on a second floor,
good luck taking a shower.

93
00:04:31,726 --> 00:04:35,056
In nineteen seventy-six, the
Navy handed the whole thing over.

94
00:04:35,366 --> 00:04:38,526
A new local agency, the Florida
Keys Aqueduct Authority,

95
00:04:38,566 --> 00:04:42,406
bought the wells, the pumps and
the pipe for seven million dollars.

96
00:04:42,954 --> 00:04:46,784
In nineteen eighty, the state
broke ground on a much bigger pipe,

97
00:04:46,954 --> 00:04:48,764
up to thirty-six inches wide.

98
00:04:49,074 --> 00:04:50,824
It was finished in nineteen eighty-two.

99
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:53,450
And that's still the lifeline today.

100
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:57,920
The water starts as rain soaking into
a layer of porous limestone under South

101
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:00,470
Florida, called the Biscayne Aquifer.

102
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:03,970
Wells in Florida City pump
it up, a plant cleans it,

103
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:05,790
and big pumps push it south.

104
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:08,670
About a hundred and thirty miles of pipe.

105
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:10,790
Forty-five bridge crossings.

106
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:15,460
Around twenty million gallons a day, for
about fifty-five thousand customers all

107
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:16,310
along the Keys.

108
00:05:16,944 --> 00:05:20,184
Basically, the Florida Keys
drink through one very,

109
00:05:20,224 --> 00:05:21,534
very long straw.

110
00:05:22,075 --> 00:05:26,105
And if you drive the Overseas Highway,
look beside some of the bridges.

111
00:05:26,555 --> 00:05:29,585
That big pipe running next
to you, over the open water?

112
00:05:29,795 --> 00:05:32,525
That's somebody's morning
shower, on its way south.

113
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:37,410
Now, here's the scary question.

114
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:41,090
What happens to one long
straw in hurricane country?

115
00:05:41,646 --> 00:05:44,756
In nineteen sixty, Hurricane
Donna hit the Keys.

116
00:05:45,166 --> 00:05:48,816
Bridges were battered, and the
water pipe broke in several places.

117
00:05:49,306 --> 00:05:52,236
It was the first time the
whole system really failed.

118
00:05:52,814 --> 00:05:54,544
So the Keys built a backup.

119
00:05:55,034 --> 00:05:59,594
In nineteen sixty-seven, the Navy opened
a seawater desalination plant on Stock

120
00:05:59,634 --> 00:06:01,664
Island, right next to Key West.

121
00:06:02,232 --> 00:06:04,402
Then came September twenty seventeen.

122
00:06:05,172 --> 00:06:08,862
Hurricane Irma, a Category Four,
smashed into the Lower Keys.

123
00:06:09,465 --> 00:06:13,675
Trees were ripped out of the ground, and
their roots tore up water lines with them.

124
00:06:13,965 --> 00:06:16,475
The water stopped flowing
down from Florida City,

125
00:06:16,705 --> 00:06:19,035
and the whole chain was
told to boil their water.

126
00:06:19,571 --> 00:06:21,741
That's when the backup plants switched on.

127
00:06:22,291 --> 00:06:26,461
Machines at Stock Island and Marathon
started turning seawater into drinking water.

128
00:06:26,972 --> 00:06:29,922
The head of the water
authority, Kirk Zuelch,

129
00:06:29,992 --> 00:06:34,832
put it simply: without that plant, Key
West couldn't have reopened as soon as it

130
00:06:34,872 --> 00:06:35,322
did.

131
00:06:35,752 --> 00:06:37,342
It was a real lifeline.

132
00:06:37,954 --> 00:06:40,264
But Irma damaged that old plant too.

133
00:06:40,494 --> 00:06:44,624
So in May twenty twenty-five, a
brand-new one opened on Stock Island.

134
00:06:44,914 --> 00:06:47,224
It's built up high and
hardened for storms,

135
00:06:47,354 --> 00:06:49,684
and it can make about four
million gallons a day.

136
00:06:50,215 --> 00:06:51,525
Normally it just waits.

137
00:06:51,835 --> 00:06:55,165
It only switches on in
emergencies, or when demand peaks.

138
00:06:55,435 --> 00:06:57,425
Like a spare tire for a whole city.

139
00:06:59,970 --> 00:07:00,560
Okay.

140
00:07:00,950 --> 00:07:02,660
The water made it all the way down.

141
00:07:02,870 --> 00:07:03,780
You took your shower.

142
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:04,760
Now what?

143
00:07:05,090 --> 00:07:05,920
Where does it go?

144
00:07:06,507 --> 00:07:09,067
For decades, the answer
in much of the Keys was:

145
00:07:09,107 --> 00:07:10,417
into the ground.

146
00:07:11,007 --> 00:07:13,907
Septic tanks and cesspits,
sitting in porous limestone,

147
00:07:13,947 --> 00:07:15,437
right next to the ocean.

148
00:07:16,052 --> 00:07:20,832
And just offshore is the only living coral
barrier reef in the continental United

149
00:07:20,872 --> 00:07:21,582
States.

150
00:07:22,012 --> 00:07:26,022
Leaking sewage feeds algae, and
algae is bad news for coral.

151
00:07:26,525 --> 00:07:28,895
So the state ordered a huge clean-up.

152
00:07:29,345 --> 00:07:32,475
Tens of thousands of septic
tanks and cesspits were removed,

153
00:07:32,725 --> 00:07:36,785
and today about ninety-four percent of
homes in Monroe County are hooked up to

154
00:07:36,825 --> 00:07:39,075
central sewers with advanced treatment.

155
00:07:39,601 --> 00:07:41,511
And the pipe itself is getting old.

156
00:07:41,981 --> 00:07:44,271
Saltwater and tides eat at buried steel.

157
00:07:44,681 --> 00:07:47,531
So the water authority is
replacing the entire line,

158
00:07:47,741 --> 00:07:50,831
section by section, over the
next twenty to thirty years.

159
00:07:51,471 --> 00:07:52,141
The price?

160
00:07:52,611 --> 00:07:54,561
Roughly ten million dollars a mile.

161
00:07:54,991 --> 00:07:57,781
The first four miles alone, in Islamorada,

162
00:07:57,891 --> 00:07:59,481
cost about forty-two million.

163
00:07:59,951 --> 00:08:03,641
And at some bridges, the new
pipe goes deep under the seabed,

164
00:08:03,771 --> 00:08:04,781
safe from storms.

165
00:08:05,430 --> 00:08:07,620
And the straw is only getting busier.

166
00:08:08,150 --> 00:08:12,040
Key West alone welcomed more than half
a million cruise passengers in twenty

167
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:12,600
twenty-four.

168
00:08:13,370 --> 00:08:17,920
Every hotel room, every restaurant, every
ice machine drinks from the same pipe.

169
00:08:18,463 --> 00:08:22,213
So next time you're in Key West,
watching the sunset at Mallory Square,

170
00:08:22,583 --> 00:08:23,353
think about this.

171
00:08:23,901 --> 00:08:27,651
The water in your hotel shower
fell as rain over the Everglades,

172
00:08:27,721 --> 00:08:30,531
soaked into the rock, got
pumped up in Florida City,

173
00:08:30,721 --> 00:08:32,961
and then traveled a hundred
and thirty miles south,

174
00:08:33,001 --> 00:08:36,831
across the sea, on forty-five
bridges, just to reach you.

175
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:41,810
For more than eighty years, one long
pipe has kept the end of the road alive.

176
00:08:42,448 --> 00:08:45,748
Would you live somewhere where all
your water comes through a single pipe,

177
00:08:45,788 --> 00:08:47,278
in hurricane country?

178
00:08:47,588 --> 00:08:48,538
Tell me in the comments.

179
00:08:49,148 --> 00:08:53,168
And if you liked this, there are more
strange places on this channel that really

180
00:08:53,208 --> 00:08:54,798
shouldn't work, but do.
